steps.body:
An `if` runs its block only when the condition is true. Add `else` to say what happens otherwise. Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never both and never neither.

```javascript
const temp = 30;
if (temp > 25) {
  console.log("Hot");
} else {
  console.log("Mild");
}
```

`else` has no condition of its own: it simply catches every case the `if` did not. Keep the braces on both blocks, and write `} else {` on one line.

When a second question only makes sense after the first answer, put an `if` inside a branch. This is called nesting. The inner check runs only when its outer branch was chosen.

```javascript harness
const loggedIn = true;
const isAdmin = true;
{{code}}
```

```javascript
if (loggedIn) {
  if (isAdmin) {
    console.log("Admin panel");
  }
}
```

Each nested block is indented two more spaces so you can see which branch it belongs to.

example.code:
const n = 7;

if (n % 2 === 0) {
  console.log(`${n} is even`);
} else {
  console.log(`${n} is odd`);
  if (n > 5) {
    console.log("and greater than 5");
  }
}

