steps.body:
When there are more than two outcomes, chain conditions with `else if`. JavaScript checks them top to bottom and runs the first block whose condition is true, then skips the rest. A final `else` catches everything no condition matched.

```javascript harness
const speed = 90;
{{code}}
```

```javascript
if (speed > 120) {
  console.log("Too fast");
} else if (speed > 60) {
  console.log("Cruising");
} else {
  console.log("Slow");
}
```

Because the first match wins, order matters. Put the most specific condition, the one that matches the narrowest range, first. With `>` or `>=` that means the highest threshold first. With `<` or `<=` it means the lowest. Reverse the order and the broad check swallows everything:

```javascript
const speed = 150;
if (speed > 60) {
  console.log("Cruising"); // runs, wrong
} else if (speed > 120) {
  console.log("Too fast"); // never reached
}
```

A common pattern is to declare `let result;` first, assign it in each branch, and print once after the chain.

example.code:
const score = 83;
let grade;

if (score >= 90) {
  grade = "A";
} else if (score >= 80) {
  grade = "B";
} else if (score >= 70) {
  grade = "C";
} else if (score >= 60) {
  grade = "D";
} else {
  grade = "F";
}

console.log(`Grade: ${grade}`);

