steps.body:
A condition does not have to be a boolean. JavaScript converts any value to true or false. These values are **falsy**: `false`, `0`, `-0`, `""`, `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`. (A later lesson adds one more, BigInt's `0n`.) Every other value is **truthy**, including `"0"`, `"false"`, and `" "` (a string holding a space).

So `if (value)` asks "is this truthy?", a short way to test for a missing or empty value:

```javascript
const nickname = "";
if (nickname) {
  console.log(nickname);
} else {
  console.log("No nickname");
}
```

To see the conversion yourself, use `Boolean(value)` or the double negation `!!value`. The first `!` flips to the opposite boolean, the second flips it back.

Gotcha: `0` and `""` are falsy even when they are real answers. With `const lives = 0;`, the check `if (lives)` treats a valid `0` as if no value was set. When `0` counts, compare explicitly, for example `if (lives !== undefined)`. The `??` operator in the ternary lesson handles this neatly.

example.code:
console.log(`0 -> ${Boolean(0)}`);
console.log(`"" -> ${Boolean("")}`);
console.log(`"0" -> ${Boolean("0")}`);
console.log(`null -> ${Boolean(null)}`);
console.log(`"hi" -> ${Boolean("hi")}`);
console.log(`NaN -> ${Boolean(NaN)}`);

const username = "";

if (username) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${username}`);
} else {
  console.log("Hello, guest");
}

